#
# The kidnapped-robot problem: teleport the body without telling the
# localizer, so AMCL's estimate is suddenly, genuinely wrong and has to
# recover from its own observations.
#
# Served as /faults/kidnap by ral_faults/fault_manager.py (Phase 4.4).
# Injection point: /world/<world>/set_pose (gz entity pose). Nothing touches
# /initialpose or /amcl_pose - resetting the filter would stage the recovery
# instead of demonstrating it.
#
# `new_pose` is in the gz WORLD frame, like every other gz entity pose, and is
# NOT converted through world_to_map.
#
# `new_pose.position.z` is IGNORED and replaced by the manager's `kidnap_z`
# parameter (0.05 m, the spawn height): the scenario schema carries x/y/yaw
# only, and a z of 0.0 would drop the wheels through the floor plane. The
# manager says so in the FaultEvent detail rather than letting the caller
# believe the z it sent was used. Linear and angular velocity are NOT zeroed,
# so the body keeps the twist it had across the teleport.
#
# Wheel odometry is continuous across the teleport (the wheels did not turn),
# which is exactly the discontinuity the estimator must survive. Metrics
# computed from ground truth must exclude the teleport step itself from path
# length; metrics.py does that explicitly and reports the excluded distance.
#
# Every component of `new_pose` must be finite; the manager refuses a NaN or
# infinite position or orientation rather than asking gz to place the body
# nowhere.
#
# `fault_id` is the join key for this fault instance: the value the caller will
# see again on every FaultEvent the manager publishes for it. The scenario
# runner sends the id it minted (<kind>_<1-based index after sorting by
# at_sec>, scenario.py:308). An EMPTY string means "allocate one", which is
# what the dashboard sends; the manager answers with <kind>_ui_<n>. The
# RESPONSE carries the id the manager used - the one sent, or the minted one
# when the request left it empty.

string fault_id
geometry_msgs/Pose new_pose
---
string fault_id
bool success
string message
